Let the beginning be small enough to walk through in an afternoon.
Let the root be spare, and let its few rooms open doors into other worlds.
Let every bin hold commands that speak argv, stdin, stdout, and stderr.
Let every lib hold what programs load in their own language.
Let every etc be a book of lines for the eye, the editor, and the machine.
Let /bin, /lib, and /etc be what the world was born with.
Let /usr be empty until a package asks for a place in it.
Let /usr/local be the work of the root user, and ~/.local the user's own.
Let devices, mutable state, and boot files belong to the world itself.
Let a workshop appear when it is needed.
Let /src hold the recipes and the source of the commands in our hands.
Let Toybox be something we can read, change, build, and put back in /bin.
Let the shell begin as sh and let Bash be a choice.
Let a Makefile be a little map, and let Tab show the things we can do.
Let there be Arch, Gentoo, and Nix, each in its own root when called.
Let their tools, libraries, databases, and stores stay in their worlds.
Let hard links and soft links choose which work appears in a prefix.
Let both versions live when we change our minds about which name to use.
Let a foreign command find its own loader in its own world.
Let a checkout become an ebuild, a package image, and a set of links.
Let an ordinary command and a line of sudocode do the same work.
Let there be gen, lord, nsh, and @ in the beginning.
Let lord think deeply and act as whoever calls, under the usual Unix rules.
Let a real question be asked when an answer changes what should happen.
Let the same little world wake on a console or inside a chroot.
Let it be like early Unix, the BSD ports, and a tiny Linux From Scratch.
Let its smallness be the beginning of what it can become.
